DATATYPES IN PSQL : 

1 - Standard PostgreSQL practice for primary key IDs (9,223,372,036,854,775,807$)
id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY.

2 - Standard PostgreSQL practice for primary key IDs (2,147,483,647)
id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY.

3 - INT / INTEGER
Stores standard whole numbers without decimals.
  stock_quantity INT,
  user_age INTEGER

4 - BIGINT
Stores huge whole numbers when INT is too small.
  views_count BIGINT


Ranges: INT and SERIAL share the same range (up to 2.14 billion). BIGINT and BIGSERIAL share the same range (up to 9.22 quintillion).

INT / BIGINT: You must provide the number manually in your INSERT statement. PostgreSQL will never auto-generate values for them.

SERIAL / BIGSERIAL: You do not need to provide a number because PostgreSQL automatically generates it by default.
However, if you choose to enter a number manually, PostgreSQL will process it normally.


5 - VARCHAR(n)
Stores text up to a fixed maximum length (n).
  username VARCHAR(30)

6. TEXT
Stores text of any length without any limit.
    content TEXT

7- BOOLEAN
Stores true/false logic.
  is_active BOOLEAN


8. DATE
Stores year, month, and day only (no time).
    date_of_birth DATE
Example value: '2005-04-15'

9. TIMESTAMP
Stores both date and exact time.
    created_at TIMESTAMP
Example value: '2026-08-29 15:30:45'

10. NUMERIC / DECIMAL
Stores exact decimals for money so there are no rounding errors.
    price NUMERIC(8, 2) -- 8 digits total, 2 after the decimal point
Example value: 29.99

